# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Velvet — frontend environment
#
# The frontend holds no third-party secrets. Every catalogue, AI, upload and
# payment credential lives in backend/.env, because the browser must never see
# them — see backend/.env.example for that list.
#
# The only value the browser needs is where to find the API.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Base URL of the Velvet API. Must match the backend's PORT.
#
# ENFORCED: next.config.mjs fails the BUILD when this is missing and
# NODE_ENV=production, because a production build without it ships an app that
# asks every visitor's own machine for data. Value: your deployed API origin,
# no trailing slash. See DEPLOY.md.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ is deliberate here: the browser makes these calls directly, and
# a public URL is not a secret. It is also the Socket.io origin for messaging.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The frontend's own origin is NOT configured here, and that is deliberate.
#
# It is fixed at http://localhost:3000 by `npm run dev`, which refuses to start
# on any other port (scripts/dev.mjs). Nothing in the frontend builds an
# absolute URL to itself — links are relative, and the backend owns the one
# place a full frontend URL is needed (FRONTEND_URL, for verification emails
# and CORS). A second source of truth here would only be able to disagree.
#
# There is no NEXTAUTH_URL / AUTH_URL either: Velvet does not use NextAuth.
# OAuth runs in Express so the client secret never reaches the browser — see
# backend/.env.example.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ── deployment: Vercel (project root = repo root) ─────────────────────────
#
# One variable, set in Project Settings → Environment Variables:
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://your-api.up.railway.app
#
# No trailing slash — the client strips one, but the Socket.io origin is
# compared as given.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at BUILD time, not read at runtime. Changing it in
# the dashboard therefore does nothing until you redeploy — and a stale value
# survives in the build cache, which is a genuinely confusing failure: the app
# calls an old API host and every request dies at the network layer with
# nothing in the server logs. If you change it, redeploy without the cache.
#
# No vercel.json is needed or wanted: Vercel detects Next.js from package.json,
# and a config file here would be one more thing that can disagree with the
# framework. (This project is Next 14, not 15 — same detection either way.)
#
# The frontend's own origin is still NOT configured here. In production it is
# whatever Vercel serves it on; nothing in the frontend builds an absolute URL
# to itself, and the backend owns the one place a full frontend URL is needed —
# FRONTEND_URL, which you set on Railway to the Vercel URL once it exists.
